---
/**
 * The August 8 change, shown: the insignia bare, then the same insignia carrying
 * the Practice diagram's labels.
 *
 * Both stages render the REAL <Insignia>, not a copy, so this figure cannot drift
 * from the mark it documents.
 *
 * The sizes are not arbitrary. Insignia has two viewBoxes — `52 50 296 296`
 * tightened to the circles, and the Venn's `0 0 400 386` which reserves the label
 * margin. The circles occupy the same 296 user units in both, so rendering the bare
 * mark at 296px and the labelled one at 400px puts the circles at an identical
 * 296px on screen. Only the labels are added; nothing moves or resizes. Any other
 * pair of sizes would quietly make the geometry look like it changed, which is the
 * one thing this figure exists to show it did not.
 */
import Insignia from './Insignia.astro';
---

<figure class="insignia-transition">
  <div class="it-row">
    <div class="it-stage">
      <div class="it-mark is-bare">
        <Insignia size={296} label="The homepage insignia before August 8 — three overlapping circles, unlabelled" />
      </div>
      <p class="telemetry it-caption">before — the mark alone</p>
    </div>

    <div class="it-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 64 24" width="64" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
        <line x1="2" y1="12" x2="55" y2="12" />
        <polyline points="46,4 55,12 46,20" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="it-stage">
      <div class="it-mark is-labelled">
        <Insignia size={400} labels label="The homepage insignia after August 8 — the same three circles, now labelled Learning Design, Engineering and Building, and Leadership and Governance, around a learning engineer centre" />
      </div>
      <p class="telemetry it-caption">after — the Practice page's labels</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The same three circles, before and after the eighth. The geometry is identical —
    same radii, same centres, same colours. The bare mark uses a viewBox tightened to
    the circles; the labelled one uses the Practice diagram's wider box, which reserves
    the margin the domain names sit in. Nothing moved. The words were the whole change.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .insignia-transition {
    margin: var(--space-16) 0 var(--space-8);
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /*
   * The labelled mark is 400px at full size and the pair needs ~760px, which does
   * not fit the 48rem prose column. Above 60rem the figure breaks out 4rem each
   * side; below it, the row becomes a column and the arrow turns to match.
   */
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .insignia-transition {
      width: 56rem;
      margin-inline: calc((48rem - 56rem) / 2);
    }
  }

  .it-row {
    /* One variable drives both stages, so the circles stay the same size as the
       figure scales down. Bare = --mark, labelled = --mark * 400/296. */
    --mark: 296px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  .it-stage {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .it-mark.is-bare :global(svg.insignia) {
    width: var(--mark);
    height: auto;
  }

  .it-mark.is-labelled :global(svg.insignia) {
    width: calc(var(--mark) * 400 / 296);
    height: auto;
  }

  .it-caption {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  .it-arrow {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  @media (max-width: 59.999rem) {
    .it-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }

    .it-arrow svg {
      transform: rotate(90deg);
    }
  }

  /* Below the labels' legibility floor the type gets small, but shrinking is still
     better than a figure that overflows the viewport. */
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .it-row {
      --mark: min(240px, 62vw);
    }
  }
</style>
